Fraunhofer FDK AAC (not included; loaded from the system if installed)
----------------------------------------------------------------------
AAC audio codec library, used for AAC+ (HE-AAC v1 and v2).

License: Fraunhofer FDK AAC Codec Library for Android (BSD-style with patent disclaimer)
Source:  https://github.com/mstorsjo/fdk-aac
SPDX:   FDK-AAC

Radiotrope does not contain, bundle or link this library: neither its source
nor its binaries include any FDK AAC code. If the user's system has
libfdk-aac installed, Radiotrope loads it at run time through a small binding
of its own (crates/radiotrope/src/audio/aac/fdk.rs) and decodes AAC with it;
otherwise it uses Symphonia's AAC-LC decoder. Building with
`--no-default-features` (without the `system-fdk` feature) leaves the loader
out as well.

The library, where a user installs it, is under the Fraunhofer FDK AAC
license, which includes the following notice:

  "NO EXPRESS OR IMPLIED LICENSES TO ANY PATENT CLAIMS, including without
   limitation the patents of Fraunhofer, ARE GRANTED BY THIS SOFTWARE LICENSE."

This means no patent license is provided for AAC encoding or decoding. Those
who install or distribute FDK AAC should be aware of potential patent
obligations in their jurisdiction.

The Free Software Foundation considers the FDK AAC license incompatible with
the GPL. Radiotrope's licensors therefore grant an additional permission under
GPL version 3 section 7 to combine Radiotrope with libfdk-aac; see the License
section of README.md. It covers the combination made on a user's system at
run time.

The tests build FDK's encoder (the fdk-aac crate, a dev-dependency) to make
AAC test streams. It is not part of any program that is built or distributed.

Full license text: https://github.com/mstorsjo/fdk-aac/blob/master/NOTICE


